3 Key Market Drivers
Transmission Grid Modernization: Aging electricity infrastructure and rising electricity demand are encouraging utilities to reinforce and expand transmission networks.
Offshore Wind and Renewable Integration: New renewable generation requires high-capacity transmission connections between generation sites and load centers.
HVDC Interconnection Projects: Long-distance and cross-border electricity transmission is increasing demand for high-voltage direct-current cable systems.

Vertical Industry Watch
The underground cable industry is benefiting from structural changes in global electricity networks. Renewable-energy development is shifting generation toward remote and offshore locations, increasing the need for high-capacity transmission connections. Urbanization is also supporting underground cable deployment because dense metropolitan areas can face land availability and right-of-way constraints. HVDC technology is becoming increasingly important for long-distance transmission and cross-border interconnection projects. At the same time, utilities are placing greater emphasis on network resilience, reliability and modernization. The market remains technically demanding because underground cable projects require extensive engineering, route planning, thermal analysis, installation expertise and specialized testing.

